// The app map: what Hearth is and how its pieces fit, for both engines (Claude and Astra) when they direct a tool.
// Read on demand only (three_do { cmd: "help", topic } in the Lab, hearth_help { topic } elsewhere); the system
// prompt carries one short line pointing here (LINE, ≈ 60 tokens), so nothing grows on every message.
// Keep each topic short and factual: it is what a fresh engine reads before picking up the owner's work.

const TOPICS = {
  app: `HEARTH is the owner's desktop app: one window for their AI agents. Native chats run Claude (Claude Code) and Astra (Codex / ChatGPT) on the owner's own subscriptions. Directors are chats docked next to a tool: the Three Director beside the Three.js Lab, Forge Debug beside their game. Videos are made in the Lab sequence and rendered to files (Hearth has no video player of its own: files open in the system player).
  Each director chat owns its own Lab scene: your tool calls reach your chat's scene (on screen, or backstage while the owner looks at another chat).
  Claude and Astra are interchangeable directors: same tools, same guide, same chat, same scene. Hearth keeps the chat's task state for whoever runs next (topic handoff).
  Everything is drivable by chat commands (/name args); suggest them to the owner, run Lab ones yourself (topic commands). Every on / off lives in Settings too, with its cost: /settings <words> opens it filtered (e.g. /settings tokens).
  Words the app uses: a sketch is a Lab piece you save (Your sketches); a scene is the sketch a chat or a sequence clip shows; render = a video or picture file made from the Lab; export = data saved as a file (a chat, notes, usage).
  The owner doesn't write code; they describe, then shape results with sliders, looks, frame sizes and the timeline. They want few choices: decide small things yourself.
  Topics: lab, nodes, react, commands, handoff, habits, sequence, motion, comp, make, board, capture, adobe, publish, apps, then the Lab's own (layers, sliders, keyframes, timeline, filters, looks, audio, refs, notes, scene, live, games, frame, bigcode).`,
  lab: `THE LAB'S MODEL (After Effects with code), bottom up:
  sketch (one per director chat; the owner's other sketches stay untouched)
  → layers, stacked bottom first; each is its own three.js module (renderer, scene, camera), with opacity, blend, x / y / scale / rotate and an in / out time on the song with fades. Filter layers (ascii, vhs, glitch, crt, glow…) restyle every layer below them.
  → per layer: tweak() sliders (the owner's knobs: Save, Shuffle, undo, ♪ links to the music), keyframes on slider values and layer settings, looks (saved slider sets, morphed by cues).
  → node graphs: the Nodes view (Alt+N) shows a layer as nodes + wires; the graph compiles to the layer's code and its knobs are the layer's sliders (topic nodes).
  → the timeline: one clock per scene. The song (beat grid, kick / snare / hit markers), else the scene's OWN timeline (no song: a length, 10 s by default, frame-exact at 30 fps; keyframes, cues, the loop and timeline edits all work on it, nothing reacts to music). Named cues (Intro, Drop…), layer tracks with keyframe lanes. three_do timeline_edit { length: 20 } sets the own timeline's length; a song loaded later keeps every keyframe and cue at its seconds (timeline_edit { snapToBars: true } puts them on its bars). The scene's sequence starts with the scene as its clip.
  → a NEW chat's scene is the orb: two node layers (Backdrop, Orb: Timeline → Glowing orb + Orb halo + Camera) in the chat's color, keyframes already on the Orb layer's sliders (size, glow, camera distance / height). Change it with three_nodes and keyframes; make it react only when asked.
  → video footage instead of (or with) a song: exact frames (media.frame, media.onFrame), a timecode counter, a cut list the sketch plays (topic footage).
  → the Lab sequence: several scenes, footage and titles on one video timeline, played in the preview and rendered frame by frame (topic sequence).
  The owner's habits: Save and Shuffle sliders constantly, switch social frame sizes (9:16, 1:1, 4:5, 16:9), Freeze frames, Present, record.
  Starting points (the gallery: the owner's New, ◎ "Start from something like…"): 26 built-in music scenes by mood (outrun sunset, hyper tunnel, chrome blob, beat burst, kinetic opener…), his scenes, history, the library. You: three_do { cmd: "run", command: "/gallery start chrome blob" } (a new scene) or "/gallery layer beat burst" (its layers on top); "/gallery list" names them.
  Describe it (round 29; the gallery search, the empty Lab, ◎ "Make it from my words", ✦ More › Look & motion): a sentence → 3 scene picks matched locally, 0 tokens (starting points + palette + ◐ look + speed / intensity + a music reaction on the sliders). You: three_do { cmd: "run", command: "/describe make <words>" } (the first pick as a new scene) · "/describe list <words>".
  Packs (Sketch ▾ › Share, ✦ More › ⇪ Share, the gallery's right-click, a .hearthpack dropped on Hearth): a scene (or its sequence) and every file it uses as one file to share; opening one shows a card first, then it lands as a new scene. You: three_do { cmd: "run", command: "/pack save" } (the owner picks where) · "/pack open <file.hearthpack>" (the owner confirms on the card).`,
  nodes: `NODES (three_nodes { command }): build visual layers as node graphs so the owner sees what you made in the Nodes view.
  "layer <preset>" adds a NEW layer from a preset (presets [word] lists them: ⏱ = timeline-driven, ♪ = reacts to music) · "preset <preset>" replaces the selected layer · "add <type> k=v… to=node.input" · "link a.out b.in" · "unlink b.in" · "set <node> k=v…" · "rm <node…>" · "list" (the graph, changed values, wires) · "types [word]" (types with their ports) · "layout" · "rebuild".
  Categories: Objects (mesh, particles, tunnel, gridCopies, ringCopies, blob, wavePlane, imagePlane, spectrumBars…), Shapes, Materials, Lights, Motion (spin, move, orbit, bounce, wobble, pulse…), Camera, Time (timeline = the scene's own clock, keys = keyframes on it, time, lfo, noiseWave, ramp), orb / orbHalo / backdrop (the new-chat orb), Layer (layerTime), Music + Triggers (only when asked to react), Math, Colors (palette, mixColors, rainbow…), Post (bloom, trails, rgbShift, film, kaleido…), Output.
  Change a node layer with three_nodes: a code edit makes its graph read-only. What nodes can't express goes in a code layer of its own (three_add_layer).`,
  react: `MUSIC REACTIVITY (only when the owner asks: "make it react", "hit on the kick", "sync it to the beat"):
  Fast way: three_do { cmd: "run", command: "/make-it-react" } links sensible sliders to the kick, bass and loudness by their names ("/make-it-react undo" takes it back).
  Nodes: wire Music nodes into what should move (hits.kick → peak → pulse.amount, levels.bass → smooth → remap → mesh.scale).
  Code: the audio globals (help audio); build hits on the owner's markers (audio.kick / snare / hit) when the song has them.
  Until then, scenes move on the timeline: time, keyframes, easing, cues and sections (help keyframes, help timeline).`,
  commands: `CHAT COMMANDS: the owner types /name args. Offer them as <suggest>/name …</suggest>; run Lab, music, nodes and sequence ones yourself with three_do { cmd: "run", command: "/name args" } (results come back as text).
  Lab: /save-look, /shuffle [colors], /fix-errors, /size 9:16, /freeze, /present, /record, /layer.
  Music: /make-it-react [undo], /analyze, /drops next, /downbeat, /auto-preset.
  Footage: /footage [go f120|step n|cut|delete|scenes], /match-pacing <clip>, /ref-frames <clip>.
  Nodes: /nodes, /nodes-layer <preset>, /nodes-presets.
  Director: /undo-edit, /redo-edit (you: three_do undo; three_edit_code safe: true), /director-engine claude|astra, /handoff, /task, /director-cost, /director-mode lean|full, /nodes-director on|off.
  Together: /jam (Claude ⇄ Astra build a visual in turns), /opinion (second opinion), /crew 3 <brief> (2–6 helpers explore variations of a scene in parallel; cost shown first; pick, undo, keep; three_do crew when /crew on).
  Sequence: /sequence, /sequence-render. Music video from the loaded song: 🎬 by the song name, a plan per section (scene, bars, transition on the beat, look, lyrics) as an editable strip, then Make it / Render (three_do autoedit; /music-video). Everything else: /help <area>.
  Live (VJ): /stage [show|go|cue|set add|output|midi learn|lfo] (set list, crossfade on the bar, MIDI, the output on a second screen).
  Phone remote: /remote on|off|devices (the owner's phone on the same Wi-Fi plays sliders, pads, the set list; pairing code shown on the computer).
  Other tools: /osc (TouchDesigner, Ableton, Resolume, TouchOSC drive sliders / pads / scenes; beat, kick, features out), /midi-out (clock, notes on beats, slider CC), /obs (transparent Browser Source page; OBS scenes follow the Lab, record; streaming asks the owner), /link (Ableton Link tempo).
  History (time machine, Alt+H): /history [list|compare n|restore n|pick colors|sliders|layer <name> n] (every version of the scene with who made it; the owner scrubs it on the preview), /back n [claude|astra] (before the director's last n turns), /branch [name].
  Audio: /audio (what the Lab hears), /audio-in [list|loopback|<device>|monitor on], /react-to <slider> <sound> [amount attack= release=], /react-preset <pump on kick…>, /react-curve <slider>, /song-features (key, builds, drops). /stems: the song split into its parts once (audio.stems.drums / bass / vocals; sliders follow "the drums").
  Flows (help flows): most commands sit in the owner's flows; /flow-status says where they are, /flow-step <answer> moves one.
  Speed: /again [n|list] repeats the owner's last command or button; /resume [chat|lab|render|flow] picks up where he left off; /habits now|fix|restore; /macro learned|keys (his one-key macros).
  Commands page: /commands <name> opens the owner's page on a command (what it does, a preview, its questions one at a time; the run gets the same chat card as a flow).`,
  flows: `FLOWS (the owner's commands as small node graphs he clicks through: Doctor, Make a video, Record, Lab scene, Sequence, Board, Export, Astra & Claude, Look, Memory, and one flow per area holding every other command). A run has steps (a command, a choice, the owner's words, an AI step, a check, a result) and a status: running, waiting for you, waiting for AI, hung (pick up here), done. Read where the chat's flows are with /flow-status (three_do { cmd: "run", command: "/flow-status" } in the Lab); when the owner asks you to move one: /flow-step <option value or words> | resume | again | refine <what> | back <n> [answer]. In a plain chat, end your reply with <flow answer="…"/> (or resume="1") instead: Hearth reads and strips it. Only commands in the "/" menu are principal; every other one still runs typed by name.`,
  handoff: `HANDOFF: Hearth (not the model) keeps each director chat's task state: the goal, recent asks, what was done (layers added, edits, nodes, keyframes…), the scene's layers, open todos and the last screenshot. Whichever engine runs next in the chat gets it once as "[Task state…]" (after /director-engine, /handoff or the ⚇ menu's switch), so Claude can pick up Astra's work and the other way round, in the same chat and scene. Read it any time: three_do { cmd: "task" }. Keep todos current with chat_progress (or three_do { cmd: "task", todo: [...] }) so the next one knows what's left.`,
  habits: `HABITS the owner asked for:
  1. Every new effect or element = a NEW layer (three_add_layer, three_nodes "layer …"), never piled into one big layer. Rewrite an existing layer (three_set_code / update_layer code) only when the owner asks to; small changes: three_edit_code.
  2. Visual layers as node graphs (three_nodes) so the Nodes view always shows what you made.
  3. Time first, like After Effects: motion from time, keyframes, easing, cues and sections; music reactivity only when asked (topic react).
  4. Knobs, not code: tweak() sliders with plain labels and groups; keep the owner's unsaved slider values.
  5. Look before claiming it works (three_screenshot small, or shot: true on an edit); fix errors first.
  6. Decide small things yourself; ask (chat_ask) only for real choices. Don't paste code: say what you made and what to ask next.`,
  capture: `CAPTURE (Hearth screenshots / records itself; reads any video's frames exactly). For the owner: one menu (Ctrl/⌘+Alt+S, or ⋯ in the rail → Capture), files in the captures folder (data/captures: shots, recordings, frames, sheets).
  Screenshots: window, tool (the screen on show), chat, transcript (the whole chat as one tall picture), dock, lab (the Lab preview at its exact frame size), a region / thing the owner drags or clicks; social frames (9:16 1080×1920, 4:5, 1:1, 16:9 and 19 more), clean (no toasts / menus / scrollbars), beautified (gradient, window bar, shadow), annotation (arrows, boxes, text, badges, blur).
  Recording: the hub page's own frame (no OS cursor, no other windows) + Hearth's own sound (mic optional; the whole computer's sound only on Windows), any fps, a tool / region / social frame, pause, markers, countdown, clicks / keys shown; the REC light is a separate window, never in the picture; WebM + MP4 (ffmpeg); opens in the system player, in Finder / Explorer, or in the Lab as media / a sequence clip.
  Tours: scripted hands-free recordings, one step per line (record, open, cmd /x, type, click, zoom, caption, title, highlight, wait, stop); /tour steps lists them, /tour intro is a 30 s app intro.
  Frame reader: exact frames by time / frame / timecode (ffprobe's true fps and packet times; VFR safe), every N, scenes, contact sheets, motion curve, pacing, palette, black / frozen / quiet stretches, loudness, letterbox, a color barcode, the best loop. /make (or capture_frames mode make) turns a video into a GIF, a trim, a timelapse, a boomerang, PNG frames for After Effects or a 9:16 / 1:1 / 4:5 copy. Reference footage is for the VIBE (pacing, motion, palette, light), never to reuse unless the owner says so.
  Commands: /shot, /rec (records Hearth anywhere; /record in the Lab records the sketch), /tour, /frames, /captures, /scenes, /contact, /pacing (see /capture-help). Tools (only when the chat opted in with /capture-tools on): capture_shot, capture_record, capture_frames, capture_list.
  Takes: the drawn cursor is smoothed (Settings › Cursor smoothing), click sounds can be mixed in (/record clicksound), a busy machine slows the take to fewer fps instead of losing it (slowedTo in the result); tours preview without recording (/tour preview <name>, capture_record { action: tour, dry: true }) with each step's time.`,
  board: `MOOD BOARD (the "Board" rail tool; Ctrl+Shift+M opens it as a drawer over any chat): the owner's references — pictures, clips, websites (live snapshots), notes, colors, frames — on an infinite canvas, several boards, a board can be linked to a chat.
  REFERENCES GIVE A VIBE, NOT FOOTAGE: take palette, light (key, contrast), color (saturation, warmth), texture (edges, grain), motion energy and cut pacing, composition, type and mood from them; never put the reference media into what you make unless the owner explicitly asks for the clip itself.
  Hearth reads each reference's vibe locally (no tokens) and sends it as short text: the owner drags a reference into the chat, or types /board-use [palette|light|motion|…], /ref <words>, /vibe. Items in a frame named "Avoid" or stamped ✕ are what NOT to do.
  With the board tools (opt-in: /board-tools on): board_list, board_vibe { board?, frame?, item?, focus?, items?, image? }, board_add { kind, text|url|colors…, frame? }, board_arrange { layout, frame? }. A board can carry a brief (the owner's intent, read first) and a default focus. Commands: /board, /board-add <url>, /board-note, /board-layout, /board-lens, /board-template, /board-export (help board lists all).
  More (board_do): collections (live saved searches + the board's moods), groups (alike refs framed, named by mood), lab-look (the board palette → the Lab sketch's palette and a saved look), timeline (each clip's shots and pace). /board-collection, /board-groups, /board-lab-look, /board-timeline.
  SEEING IT (round 14): board_look shows real pictures — no target = the whole board as one picture with item ids, item (+ region [x,y,w,h] for a close-up), items (compare: true = side by side + vibe differences), selection, frame; size small (default, 512 px; the board 768) | medium | large: start small, ask bigger only for detail. board_watch { item, count | times | mode: scenes, as: sheet | frames } = a clip or gif as timestamped frames. Name items by id (i3) in replies: the owner clicks them to jump to the item. board_do caption { item, text } keeps one line on what you saw (the vibe text carries it, so nobody needs to look again); find { query } finds items by description; pinterest { phrases | aesthetic } shows Pinterest searches as buttons (the owner picks pins in a Hearth window; the pin link stays as the item's source); board_add { kind: url, url, source, credit } adds a picture you found with its credit. (round 30) board_do pins { query? } finds images like the board's vibe (or the query) on Pinterest (opened in the owner's Hearth Chrome, signed in there) and returns a short numbered list; the owner sees them as a picker under your reply and ticks the ones that go on the board (saved locally, their link as source). Nothing is added before they pick. The owner's own way: board ⋯ / right-click › Find images like this…, or the ◎ mission Find images for this vibe. The owner can also show pictures to you: right-click › Show to a chat, Alt+drop from the drawer, /board look | watch | compare | find | pinterest.`,
  sequence: `THE LAB SEQUENCE ("▤ Sequence" on the Lab timeline, /sequence): a video built in the Lab from scenes. Main track: scene clips (a sketch / chat scene, its own look or a board vibe, playing on its own clock from the clip's start) and footage clips, with transitions between them (dissolve by default); tracks: Titles (styles + animations), Overlays (a layer or filter over a range), Sound (the song: its analysis still drives what reacts; scenes stay time-driven unless the owner asks).
  The preview plays it live, switching / cross-fading scenes in the same page; seek is frame-exact (any scene, any frame). Render = frame by frame at 9:16 / 16:9 / 1:1 / 4:5 with the sound, into a file (the render queue; the owner opens it in the system player). Adobe's Premiere entry takes it as FCP XML.
  Tool: three_do { cmd: "sequence", op } — status · add { scene: name | number | "current", at: "bar 9" | seconds | "f120", bars | secs, look } · add { title | footage (path) | overlay (layer / filter) | song } · transition { clip: n, type: dissolve | dip-black | wipe-left | push-left | zoom-in | cut… } (no clip: every cut) · length { clip, bars | secs } · trim { clip, edge, to } · split { at } · delete { clip, ripple } · move { clip, to } · look { clip, name } · fit (cuts on bars) · frame { at } (a picture) · render { format } · undo. Clip n = its place on the main track; titles are "Titles.1".
  Defaults need no choices: a scene lasts 4 bars with a song (4 s without), a dissolve between scenes, new scenes fit the bars. Owner's commands: /sequence, /add-scene, /sequence-render.
  Editing footage and sound (labcut): reframe { clip(s), frame: "from left to right" | "right third" | "zoom 120%" | fit | blur | reset } is per output format (all: every format); motion { move: push-in | pan-left… | auto } (pictures push in by default); volume / fade { edge, secs }; tracks Song · Voice (add { voice: path }, the music ducks under it: duck { on, amount }) · SFX (add { sfx }); grade { look } a per-clip color look (all: the whole sequence); chop { every: bar | beat } cuts on the song's bars; captions { path .srt | lines }, caption_look, lower { text: "Name — Role" }; outputs { formats: socials } + render_outputs = one file per format, each with its own framing (swap the frame size to frame a format). Renders write the Captions track as an .srt too.
  Each scene owns its sequence (switching scene / chat switches it; ops act on the director chat's scene; of: another scene). Fewer decisions: arrange { template: music-video | product-intro | teaser | bumper | loop | lyric (lines) | changelog (lines) | reel | mood | drop-showcase, scenes, secs } puts the scenes on the song's sections (cuts on bars, a transition chosen per section change, a scene coming back in another look); again = another arrangement; versions { secs: [15, 6] } = shorter cuts; fill { clip } fills a gap; vary { clip } / vary_sections / swap_looks = per-clip looks; nest { name } = a sequence as a clip; copy / paste { into } across sequences; roll / slide / marker. Lab transitions: lab-fly, lab-fly-back (each scene's camera flies), lab-morph (shared layers stay), lab-depth, lab-depth-far, lab-mosh, lab-stutter, lab-match (match cut on the shape), lab-flash-beat, lab-bloom. A scene's own hit markers and cue looks move with its clip, like keyframes. Owner: /arrange, /sequence make | again | versions | fill | vary.`,
  motion: `MOTION DESIGN KIT (for the owner's Hearth intro for socials; the camera as an INPUT is help webcam): ready layers drawn by motion.<kind>(P) in the sandbox, each one node in the Nodes view, every knob a slider (keyframe them). Time-driven from the layer's own start; words land on the song's hit markers / beats only when there is a song.
  Families: Hearth on screen (ui: captures of Hearth or Hearth drawn — mock:chat, mock:lab, mock:board, mock:nodes, mock:jam — as 3D cards / glass panels / laptop, phone, browser, tablet frames; layouts single, stack, parallax, explode, fly-in, carousel, wall, hero, zoom, fan, feed, split, turntable, elements; plus a clicking cursor), kinetic type (33 per-letter animations: type-on, cascade, split, slam, wave, scramble, breathe, word-by-word…, fitted to the frame's safe zone), 3D type, camera moves (dolly, orbit, crane, truck, whip pan, rack focus, handheld, zoom punch, vertigo…: a rig the 3D layers follow, or the whole picture under it), logo reveals (the Hearth flame / Forgeheart anvil: line draw, 3D spin, slam, burst, particles, molten), brand looks (forge gradient, molten, aurora, grid, grain, light sweep, embers…), end cards (name, line, call to action).
  Tool: three_do { cmd: "motion", op } — list { family } · add { preset: "cascade" | "laptop" | "dolly in" | "flame line"…, words, pics: ["hearth-1", "mock:lab"], values: { knob: v }, in, out } (a NEW layer) · hearth { layout, frame, words } (a whole shot: backdrop, screens, cursor, title, camera) · words { layer, text } · set { layer, values } · seq { preset, at, secs } (an overlay on the Lab sequence) · intro (a sample sequence logo → on screen → end card). Pictures of Hearth: capture first (the owner's ◉ Capture or capture_shot), they become the sketch's references. Board references give vibe only. Owner's commands: /motion-kit, /hearth-on-screen, /kinetic, /camera-move, /shot-list, /logo-reveal, /end-card, /brand-look, /motion-words, /motion-seq, /motion-intro.`,
  model: `3D MODELS (round 15): a model (Meshy, Sketchfab, Mixamo, Poly Haven, Spline exports, .glb .gltf .fbx .obj .stl .ply) is a 🧊 layer whose code is lab3d.model(refs.key, P, { name, hdri: refs.sky, clips: [refs.anim] }), drawn by the sandbox: fitted to the frame and standing on the ground, progress bar while loading, clear errors for bad files, everything disposed on re-run. Every knob is a tweak() slider: Model (size, height, x, z, turn, tilt, spin = turntable, bob, explode, faceCamera, detail = simplify), Material (material: original chrome gold glass toon clay matte wireframe holographic xray normals palette; tint, glow), Animation (clip by name, play, loop, speed, blend), Camera (orbit, crane, distance, lens, camSpin, aim), Light (light: studio three-point rim neon golden sunset moody flat; lights, exposure, env, lightTurn), World (ground: none shadow mirror floor gloss grid; sky clear | hdri | color, fog), Effects (bloom, dof), Music (pulse, kickGlow; 0 unless asked).
  Camera moves (orbit, half-orbit, dolly-in, dolly-out, push-in, crane, crane-down, hero, arc, fly-by, top-down, low-angle, vertigo, reveal-spin, zoom-punch) are keyframes on those camera sliders from the playhead, on the scene's own clock, with a 🎥 cue: they play in renders and the Lab sequence; edit them with three_do keyframes.
  Tool: three_do { cmd: "model", op } — list (the model library: #n, names, clips) · add { file | item: "#2" | name | "last" } (a NEW layer) · replace { item, layer } (same sliders / keys / placement) · set { values } · material { preset, tint } · look { preset: product showroom neon golden noir hologram clay cinematic } · light { preset } · sky { query (Poly Haven words) | file | "off" } · ground { preset } · clip { name | next, speed, loop, blend } · clipfile { file } (Mixamo FBX joins the clips) · move { preset, at, secs } · clearmoves · turntable { speed } · explode { amount } · fx { fog, bloom, dof } · react { pulse, glow | off } · fit · lite { detail } · info (clips, triangles). The owner: /model <verb> (or /3d), right-click the preview → 🧊 3D model, the floating model library (drag onto the preview, Shift = replace), W / E / R gizmo.`,
  comp: `COMP (precomps, like After Effects): a precomp is a layer of your scene whose content is ANOTHER scene (another chat's, a sketch, or a Lab sequence), live: when that chat's director edits its scene, the layer here changes too. It has its own clock (start: the scene second it starts at, speed, offset, loop or hold; time remap keys = scene s → its s), the usual layer settings (x / y / scale / rotate / opacity / blend / in / out / fades, keyframable) and its own crop [t, r, b, l] %, mask (rounded, circle, ellipse), soft edge, effects (blur, bright, contrast, sat, hue, gray, invert, sepia) and resolution. Filter layers above it work on it. Precomps nest; a scene can't hold itself.
  Tool: three_do { cmd: "comp", op } — list · add { source: chat title | sketch name | "seq:<name>", layout: full | pip | left | right | top | bottom | q1-q4 | circle } · set { layer, … } · key { layer, property: remap | cropT… | blur… | x | scale…, keys: [{ time, value, ease }] } · remove · render { format } (frame by frame).
  Parts at once: dispatch { parts: [{ brief, engine: claude | astra, name }], layout: time (one after the other, default) | split (side by side) | stack } gives each part its own director chat and scene, all working at once (each gets only its brief); they show here as precomps. parts (status: working / waiting / done / stuck, what each said) · feedback { part, text } · again { part, fresh } · swap { part } (Claude ⇄ Astra). Split a video into parts when the owner asks for one made by several agents; check parts, give feedback, then render.
  The owner: /comp, /dispatch a | astra: b | c, Alt+C in the Lab, right-click a layer → ◫ Precomp.`,
  chats: `CHATS (round 9): what your tool calls make shows under your reply as cards the owner opens, scrubs and drags to the board or another chat (captures and renders as files, Lab frames you looked at, the scene you changed, a sequence, a board item). Hearth draws them from its own record of your calls: you don't need to describe or link files; name what you made in a few words. (round 28) Every change you make (Lab scenes, the board, files, settings you change by a command) is kept in one timeline the owner can undo: "↶ Undo what it did" under your reply, or Activity › Changes by AIs (per change, or everything since one). If the owner asks to undo what you did, point them there (or they run /ai-undo reply) instead of reverting by hand.
  Each chat remembers the board, scene, sequence, captures and renders it worked with. When the owner's words point at one ("use the board", "render it again", "the last capture"), the message ends with one line like [Hearth context: board "Neon" (linked to this chat); Lab sequence "Intro"; last video /path.mp4]: that is what "it" means; use your tools on it (and a board vibe attached as <file name="vibe · …"> is a vibe, never footage). Chats continued on the other engine (handoff, continue with) keep the same context. (round 23) The owner may talk to you by voice (push to talk, transcribed locally): a spoken message can be loose or lack punctuation; your reply to it is read aloud (code blocks are skipped), so lead with the answer in plain sentences. Short spoken commands ("freeze", "save", "next scene") run in Hearth without reaching you.`,
  make: `MAKES (round 11): everything Hearth creates for the owner is a "make" with ONE name, color and mark: its rooms (a Lab chat, a Sequence chat, a board frame…) are titled "<Name> · Lab", "<Name> · Sequence", grouped under the make in the chats list, made AHEAD of time when a creation has parts (the owner can open them before anything is in them: "planned · waiting for Lab"). A /dispatch comp, a jam, a run that renders or records are makes too. In a room, name what you make "<Name> · <room>" (the line [Make “…”] tells you) and say when your part is done: the next room takes it from there.
  Tool: three_do { cmd: "make", op } — plan { text: "a three.js animation that cuts into a video", name? } (rooms made now; returns them) · list · status { make }. The owner: /makes, /makes plan <words>, the Makes list on the Commands page.`,
  projects: `PROJECTS (round 23): a project groups one piece of the owner's work: chats of any agent, Lab scenes (and their sequences), boards, renders, captures, workspace files, downloads, deploys; it has a name, mark, color, cover, notes and a status. Made by hand (/project new <name>: the chat it's run from comes along), from a make with rooms (automatically, same identity), or from a suggestion (/project suggest: shared names + links + timing, no tokens). A project chat's scene, and screenshots / renders made while it's in front of the owner, join it by themselves. Home (/home) shows projects as cards with "continue where you left off"; /everything <words> (Ctrl/⌘+Shift+K) searches projects, chats + messages, scenes (names, code, sliders), boards (items), renders, files, commands. To file something: /project add [chat|scene|board|render|<path>] [to <project>]. The chats list groups chats by project (/project group on|off, /project filter <project>).`,
  sync: `SYNC (round 10): the owner's Mac and PC share chats, scenes, sketches, sequences, boards (+ media), notes, memory, prompts, portable settings, captures and renders through a cloud drive folder they already use (iCloud Drive / Google Drive / Dropbox / OneDrive): <drive>/Hearth/. Hearth always runs from its local data folder; the sync engine copies changes both ways and merges (chat messages and scene fields merge; a field changed two ways is a "conflict", both versions kept). Offline is normal. Paths are stored relative to the data folder, so attachments open on both computers. The owner checks it with /sync status (or the dot at the bottom of the rail); /sync now, /sync conflicts, /sync trash. Nothing for you to do: don't write into the cloud folder yourself. (round 19) The chats' workspace (what you make there) syncs too; a path from the owner's other computer (…/Hearth/data/…, a Mac path on Windows or the reverse) is the same file in this computer's data folder: Hearth translates it. To show the owner a page / file you made, call chat_open { path | url, how: default | browser | reveal | preview } instead of telling them to double-click it. If the owner says Hearth feels heavy / slow / uses memory or battery: /background shows what runs in the background and the memory of each part; /apps free (or /background free) frees hidden app windows now. If it stutters or lags: /smooth (round 20) shows the real long frames and input delays measured on this computer, by area, with the code behind them; its Copy for Claude is the summary a dev session needs. (round 21) If Hearth itself breaks (an app bug, a crash, a hub tool failing, not your own code), chat_report_problem { title, details } drafts a report to Hearth's developer: a GitHub issue the owner reviews, edits and sends (/report; /report list shows sent ones and whether they're fixed).`,
  renderqueue: `RENDER QUEUE (round 13): everything Hearth renders, records or exports (Lab sequence renders and comps, recordings, presets) is one queue: ffmpeg jobs one at a time, each with a bar, time left, pause / resume / cancel / retry, its make, then a note with Open / Reveal / Copy path; failures say why with a fix. Presets: vertical (Reels / TikTok / Shorts 9:16), story, feed45, square, yt1080, yt4k, gif, webm, master (ProRes), audio; socials = vertical+feed45+square+yt1080; options fps=, quality=draft|high|best, size=full|2/3|half, fit=crop|blur|fit. The owner drives it with /renders (render <presets…> [seq | file], socials, pause|resume|cancel [n|all], retry|again [n], open|reveal|copy|trash [n], history). To render for the owner, suggest the /renders line; existing tools (three_sequence render) also land in the queue. (round 27) 📮 Ready to post: a finished video is checked for YouTube Shorts / TikTok / Reels / YouTube 16:9 / Instagram square (shape, size, length limits, bitrate, codec, loudness near -14 LUFS, detail under the app's buttons, the loop), with cover ideas, the title / description / hashtags (the owner edits them) and a post folder (video, cover, post.txt) next to the video. The owner opens it from a render's 📮 Post, the ◎ mission "Get it ready to post", or /post [file] [platform]. Hearth never uploads or signs in: don't offer to post for them.`,
  labexport: `LAB EXPORT (round 22): best-quality output of the Lab scene or the Lab sequence, rendered in a hidden export page (the preview and the app stay usable; a row of the render queue with time left, pause / resume / cancel). Frame-exact on the scene's clock, randomness seeded (the same render twice = the same pixels). Formats: mp4 (H.264), hevc (10-bit), prores (4444 + alpha), webm (VP9 + alpha), png (sequence + alpha), gif / loop (perfect loops: N bars of the song with a cross-fade, or 'detect' = the loop the frames make), still, still16 (16-bit PNG for print), glb (the 3D content at the playhead), html (the scene in one self-contained .html), preset (slider values file), sheet (contact sheet). Options: a size (frame, 1080p, 4k, 8k, 8k-square, a4, a3, poster, 2x, 3840x2160), 24–120fps, ss2–ss4 supersampling, blur / blur16 motion blur, shutter=180, draft|high|best, 10s | 4bars | 120frames, at=12.5, seq, alpha | opaque, mute. You: three_do { cmd: "run", command: "/lab-export mp4 4k ss2" } (the answer has the file's path, size, frames, speed). The owner: /lab-export (alias /lx), right-click the preview › ⇪ Export, the sequence's Render panel › Export…, Ctrl/⌘+K "Lab: Export…".`,
  shader: `SHADER LAYERS (round 23): a ◇ layer is a fragment shader: its code is shader(/* glsl */ \`…\`, P, opts). Write Shadertoy-style GLSL (void mainImage(out vec4 fragColor, in vec2 fragCoord); main() + gl_FragColor works too). Built in: iTime (the scene's clock, not the music's unless it has a song) iTimeDelta iFrame iResolution iMouse iDate iChannel0–3 iChannelResolution; music uKick uSnare uHats uHit uBeat uBass uMid uTreble uLevel uDrop uEnergy uBeatPhase uBarPhase uBands; uPrev (its last frame), uBelow (the layers under it), uAudio (512×2: spectrum y 0.25, waveform y 0.75). Sliders: declare uniforms with a hint and Hearth writes the tweak() line itself: uniform float uGlow; // [Look] 0..2, 1 Glow · vec3 / vec4 colors // #ff8c42 · bool // on · int // options: a, b · vec2 // 0..1 = 0.5 0.5 (XY pad). Channels (opts iChannel0: 'audio' | 'self' (feedback) | 'A'–'D' (buffers) | 'below' | 'layer:<name>' | 'ref:<key>' | 'noise' | 'none'); passes: opts.buffers { A: { code: bufferA, iChannel0: 'A', scale } } drawn before the image; opts.scale 0.25–1 = the layer's preview resolution; alpha: true keeps its alpha. Errors come back with their line; the last version that compiled keeps drawing. Tool: three_do { cmd: "shader", op } — list {filter} (38 starters: noise, patterns, raymarch, music, feedback, filters) · add {starter | code (GLSL, a Shadertoy paste or ISF), name, buffers, channels} (a NEW layer) · glsl {layer, pass: image|A|common, code} (replace one pass) · channel {layer, pass, channel: 0-3, source} · scale {layer, value} · alpha · buffer · info · check {code} (compile only). Starters are also templates: three_add_layer { template: "shader-tunnel" }. The owner: /shader (workbench), /shader new <starter>, /shadertoy (paste), right-click a layer › ◇ Shader, the preview's right-click › ◇ Shaders.`,
  particles: `GPU PARTICLES (round 24): a ✦ layer is GPU particles: its code is a tweak() block + particles(P, opts) (simulated in float textures on the GPU at 60 steps a second of the scene's clock: pause holds them, renders are the same every time; 100k–2M on a real GPU, fewer in the preview when frames drop). opts (JSON): emitter point|sphere|ball|disc|ring|box|plane|line|torus|spiral|text (text)|image (image: a reference key)|model (source: a 🧊 model layer, or auto; camera: 'source' follows its camera)|layer (source: another layer's bright pixels), look points|streak|mesh (mesh: tetra|octa|box|shard), attractors [[x,y,z,s]], seed. Sliders (P keys; missing ones use defaults): count (thousands) emit life size sizeEnd opacity color1-3 colorBy (life|speed|source|height|random) sprite blend (add|alpha) trail streak speed spread dirY outward swirl curl curlScale curlSpeed turb gravity wind drag vortex vortexPull attract boids form (hold the shape) dissolve sweep floor bounce emitSize emitX/Y/Z emitSpin orbit distance tilt; music kickEmit (burst on kick) kickPush (explode on kick) bassForce (forces × bass) hueBright (hue follows the spectral centroid); any slider can also follow a sound (/react-to). Tool: three_do { cmd: "particles", op } — list {filter} (30 presets: galaxy, nebula, smoke, fire, snow, rain, fireflies, sparks, kicksphere, shockwave, dissolve (text), modeldust, glowrise, ribbons, swarm…) · add {preset, name, text, source, image, sliders} (a NEW layer) · set {layer, values: {slider: value}} (in the code; adds a missing slider) · emitter {layer, kind, text, source, image} · look {layer, look} · react {layer} (the preset's music links) · info {layer}. Presets are also templates: three_add_layer { template: "particles-galaxy" }. The owner: /particles, /particles text <word>, /particles model, right-click a layer › ✦ Particles, the preview's right-click › ✦ Particles.`,
  progress: `PROGRESS BARS (round 11): everything that takes time shows a slim bar on itself (your reply being written, its chat row, the rail icon, the comp / flow / jam cards, renders) and in one list at the bottom of the rail. Hearth measures what it can (ffmpeg, frames, steps, rounds) and estimates the rest from how long the same kind of work took before (your tool calls and the text you stream count). You never need to report progress. When the owner turned it on (/progress tags on, directors only), write <progress pct="40" note="what's next"/> now and then during long work (hidden from the reply); a hub call may also carry progress: 40. /progress lists what runs; /progress learned says the usual times.`,
  adobe: `ADOBE (round 14, adobe_do when the owner turned it on with /adobe-tools): the owner has the whole Adobe suite (Mac mainly, Windows too). Hearth finds Photoshop, Illustrator, Premiere Pro, Media Encoder, InDesign and After Effects and runs ExtendScript in them (Mac: AppleScript; Windows: COM; Premiere / Media Encoder: the "Hearth bridge" panel, /adobe bridge install once). Photoshop: open {path | "lab"} (a still of the Lab), board (the mood board as a layered PSD, each picture a layer at its place), actions / action (the owner's actions), sizes (social sizes), back {to: chat|board|lab} (the front document as PNG back into Hearth; you see it). Illustrator: svg (→ SVG added as a Lab reference: refTexture / an SVGLoader). Premiere: timeline (the Lab sequence as FCP XML: footage cuts only; scenes, transitions, titles and looks are listed, render them first), import {path|"last"}. Media Encoder: queue {path|"last", preset}. InDesign: pdf, place. After Effects: import (as a comp). Files sent out are watched: when the owner saves them in the app, Hearth offers to bring them back. Missing apps answer why. The owner: /adobe, /photoshop, /premiere, /illustrator, /media-encoder, /indesign; Lab Sketch ▾ → Publish & Adobe; board right-click → Adobe. Your own scripts (action script) ask the owner first.`,
  publish: `PUBLISH (round 14, deploy_do when the owner turned it on with /deploy-tools): a Lab scene (a self-contained page: the Lab's own player + three.js copied next to it + the scene's layers and references; plays anywhere, without the song), a video (a player page), a folder (a static site) or a project (Vercel / Netlify build it) goes on Vercel, Netlify, Cloudflare Pages or GitHub Pages with the services' own CLIs (vercel, netlify, wrangler, gh with git). Flow: build / preview (a 127.0.0.1 address, a hidden-window check that it draws, a picture) → publish (the owner is ALWAYS asked first) → the address, kept in the history (rollback: Vercel / Netlify switch back; the others publish the earlier build again; remove). Sign-in: login {provider} opens the service's own sign-in in a terminal window the owner sees; never ask for passwords or tokens. Missing CLIs answer with the install line. The owner: /deploy (scene [name] [to netlify] [draft] · video last · folder <path> · preview · status · login · history · rollback · remove), Lab Sketch ▾ → Publish & Adobe, Ctrl/⌘+K "Publish the Lab scene on the web…".`,
  apps: `APPS (round 14): the owner's services, each in its own window and sign-in (Meshy, Suno, Figma, Spline, Sketchfab, Mixamo, Poly Haven, Unsplash, Coolors, Google Fonts, Runway, ElevenLabs, YouTube Studio, Instagram, TikTok, Pinterest…; /apps lists them). Downloads from an app window land in Downloads/Hearth/<App> and go where they fit: a .glb → a Lab model layer, an .hdr → the Lab's sky, a song → the Lab's song, a clip → the board (or opened in the system player), a picture → the board. MESHY makes 3D models from words or a picture (/meshy, tool meshy_generate when the agent has /apps-tools on): it COSTS the owner credits, Hearth shows the cost and the owner approves each one; models land in the owner's Meshy library (/meshy models), the Lab (a 🧊 layer: refs.<key> loaded with GLTFLoader, sliders size / height / spin / tilt / light) or the board. SUNO has no public API: /suno prompt writes style tags + lyrics from the scene's vibe and types them into Suno's boxes (apps_open { app: 'suno', paste, field }); the owner presses Create; /suno use loads the newest Suno download as the Lab's song. Free skies: /polyhaven <words> (a 🌅 bottom layer). 40+ AI apps too (image / video / 3D / music / research / site builders / editing): suggest one that fits with apps_open { app, suggest: true, why } (a card; the owner opens it), apps_open { op: 'discover' } lists what fits now and isn't tried yet; apps_open { app, send: 'board'|'lab'|'render' } puts a picture on the clipboard and opens the app to paste. CHROME (round 30): Google refuses sign-in inside Hearth, so an app or website chat can open in the owner's real Chrome (a Hearth profile Hearth drives); apps_page { app, do: text|click|type|key|shot|wait|go|info } works the same there and in Hearth's windows; the owner signs in in Chrome, never through you. (round 31) That Chrome runs out of sight: its pages show inside Hearth's own windows (a Chrome view) and the owner uses them there; Chrome's own window comes up only to sign in.`,
  studio: `STUDIO (round 15): one-click pipelines that chain Hearth's parts, each a make (rooms made now) walked as a flow run (Commands page "Going now", hung → pick up here): Board → video, Song → visualizer, Idea → 3D hero (Meshy: cost shown, the owner says yes), Scene → website (asks before publishing), Still → Photoshop, Vibe → song (Suno). /studio opens the picker (what each costs, what's missing here and how to fix it); /studio <name> [words] starts one (three_do { cmd: "run", command: "/studio song" } in the Lab, only when the owner asks); /studio list · status.`,
};

const LINE = (helpCall) => `HEARTH (the owner's app: Claude and Astra chats, the Lab, chat commands): Claude and Astra share this chat, its scene and the task state Hearth keeps. Map: ${helpCall} app|lab|nodes|react|commands|handoff|habits.`;

const ALIAS = { pipeline: 'studio', pipelines: 'studio', recipes: 'studio', recipe: 'studio', photoshop: 'adobe', premiere: 'adobe', illustrator: 'adobe', indesign: 'adobe', mediaencoder: 'adobe', ame: 'adobe', creativecloud: 'adobe', deploy: 'publish', deployment: 'publish', vercel: 'publish', netlify: 'publish', cloudflare: 'publish', githubpages: 'publish', website: 'publish', web: 'publish', hosting: 'publish', queue: 'renderqueue', rq: 'renderqueue', presets: 'renderqueue', exports: 'renderqueue', loading: 'progress', bars: 'progress', eta: 'progress', makes: 'make', rooms: 'make', project: 'projects', home: 'projects', everything: 'projects', room: 'make', plan: 'make', precomp: 'comp', precomps: 'comp', compositing: 'comp', dispatch: 'comp', parts: 'comp', cloud: 'sync', icloud: 'sync', dropbox: 'sync', drive: 'sync', onedrive: 'sync', offline: 'sync', flow: 'flows', journey: 'flows', journeys: 'flows', wizard: 'flows', steps: 'flows', kinetic: 'motion', typography: 'motion', motiondesign: 'motion', ui: 'motion', mockup: 'motion', devices: 'motion', endcard: 'motion', logo: 'motion', logos: 'motion', brand: 'motion', camera: 'motion', cameramoves: 'motion', shots: 'motion', shotlist: 'motion', seq: 'sequence', montage: 'sequence', storyboard: 'sequence', hearth: 'app', overview: 'app', program: 'app', map: 'app', model: 'lab', graph: 'nodes', node: 'nodes', reactive: 'react', reactivity: 'react', command: 'commands', cmds: 'commands', slash: 'commands', task: 'handoff', switch: 'handoff', engines: 'handoff', astra: 'handoff', claude: 'handoff', rules: 'habits', conventions: 'habits', renders: 'renderqueue', moodboard: 'board', boards: 'board', refs: 'board', references: 'board', vibe: 'board', footage: 'capture', frames: 'capture', record: 'capture', recording: 'capture', screenshot: 'capture', screenshots: 'capture', tour: 'capture', tours: 'capture', edit: 'sequence', edits: 'sequence', nle: 'sequence', cut: 'sequence', titles: 'sequence', transitions: 'sequence', video: 'sequence', editor: 'sequence', social: 'sequence', socials: 'sequence', promo: 'sequence', teaser: 'sequence', chat: 'chats', cards: 'chats', context: 'chats', attach: 'chats', attachments: 'chats', meshy: 'apps', suno: 'apps', windows: 'apps', polyhaven: 'apps', hdri: 'apps', models: 'model', glb: 'model', gltf: 'model', mixamo: 'model', sketchfab: 'model', turntable: 'model', lab3d: 'model', export: 'labexport', 'lab-export': 'labexport', lx: 'labexport', prores: 'labexport', alpha: 'labexport', supersampling: 'labexport', supersample: 'labexport', motionblur: 'labexport', gifloop: 'labexport', loops: 'labexport', print: 'labexport', '4k': 'labexport', '8k': 'labexport', still: 'labexport' };

// (round 31, perfectloop) the scene loops perfectly
TOPICS.lab += `\n  Perfect loop (round 31; ✦ More › ⇪ Make › ∞ Make it loop, ◎ "Make it loop", Ready to post's loop fix): the timeline loops over N bars (or N s) and the scene's clock wraps with it in the preview and renders; keyframes come back round; a seamless check (last frame vs first) with one-click fixes (blend the last beat into the first). You: three_do loop {bars, check, fade, render}; periodic code: help loop (loopT, loopSin, loopNoise).`;
// (round 32, labvariations) quick local variations
TOPICS.lab += `\n  Variations (round 32; ✦ More › ✨ Look & motion › 🎲 Variations, the toolbar's ⋯, ◎ "Quick variations (free)"): 6 takes on the scene made locally (no tokens) by nudging every layer's live sliders (◐ Grade ones too) and the palette, each leaning on one group: colors · motion (livelier / calmer) · camera · look; the owner clicks one (one undo step), "More like this" makes a closer set, a lock per group keeps it. You: three_do run "/variations [words]" (use N · more N · again · undo · lock|unlock colors|motion|camera|look · status · close).`;
// (round 33, multiformat) one export, every shape
TOPICS.lab += `\n  All formats (round 33; right-click the preview › ⇪ Export › ▦ All formats, ✦ More › ⇪ Make, the toolbar's ⋯, 📮 Ready to post): the scene / sequence exported as 16:9, 9:16, 1:1, 4:5 MP4s in one folder, one after another, each reframed (pan / zoom of the scene cameras) so the subject stays in frame; the owner adjusts each shape's framing on its preview. Nothing is uploaded. You: three_do run "/all-formats [9:16 1:1 … | socials] [now]" (status · cancel).`;
// (round 34, scenedoctor) a free check of the scene
TOPICS.lab += `\n  Fix my scene (round 34; ✦ More › 🩺 Fix my scene, the toolbar's ⋯, a calm hint by itself on a blank frame / a layer error): a local check (no tokens) for errors, a blank / too dark / blown-out frame, the subject off-screen / tiny / the camera inside it, NaN values, slow frames, music layers with nothing playing, missing files; one-click fixes, one undo each; with no local fix it puts a short request in your chat box (that's how such a message reaches you). For the AI: three_do run '/fix-scene check' lists them.`;
Object.assign(ALIAS, { particle: 'particles', gpuparticles: 'particles', emitter: 'particles', emitters: 'particles', gpgpu: 'particles', sparks: 'particles' }); // (round 24)
// (round 25, motion) the camera as a live Lab input (tools/three-motion-in*.js)
TOPICS.webcam = `CAMERA IN (round 25): the owner's webcam / capture card / a video file as a live Lab input, tracked on this computer (light tracker: movement, flow, a colour blob; MediaPipe once the owner downloaded it: hands, body, face, the person's mask). Off by default; op camera {on: true} asks the owner; it pauses while the Lab is off screen; nothing is recorded or sent; your screenshots are pixelated while it shows (unless the owner shares it).
  Tool: three_do { cmd: "motion", op } — camera {on: true|false, device, file, mirror, res: 480|720|1080, tracker: auto|light} (no args: status, devices) · track {names} (values now) · follow {slider, feature, amount, layer} (the slider follows it: the ♪ react-to mapping, curve editor) · look {preset} · looks. Presets: hands-particles (particles follow my hands), body-sparks, silhouette-glow, motion-trails, head-camera (head turns the camera), mouth-bloom (mouth opens the bloom), camera-bg, cutout, move-grow, pinch-zoom, hands-apart, smile-color, blink-flash, wave-speed, debug.
  Features (0..1; x left→right on screen, y bottom→top; turns 0.5 = straight): camMotion camLeft camRight camTop camBottom camCenter camX camY camFlowX camFlowY camBlob camBlobX camBlobY camBright camZ0…camZ8 (3×3 grid) · handX handY handOpen pinch hands hand2X hand2Y handSpread handSpeed · headX headY headTurn headTilt wristLX wristLY wristRX wristRY bodyX bodyY bodySize armsUp person · mouthOpen smile blink browUp faceX faceY · maskArea. Without MediaPipe, hand / body = where it moves.
  In code: webcam.feat.handX (also audio.feat.handX, audio.env('pinch', 20, 200), onHit('pinch', fn)); webcam.layer(P, {mode: 'video'|'cutout'|'silhouette'|'trails'|'mask'|'debug'}) shows it in a layer (P sliders: opacity zoom x y fit bright contrast sat hue blur color glow soft fade); webcam.texture() / webcam.maskTexture() THREE textures that follow it (shader uniforms); webcam.points (hands). Particles from the body: a 'silhouette' or 'mask' layer + particles emitter 'layer' with source = it. The owner: /webcam, right-click the preview › 📷 Camera.`;
Object.assign(ALIAS, { webcamera: 'webcam', camerain: 'webcam', tracking: 'webcam', bodytracking: 'webcam', handtracking: 'webcam', facetracking: 'webcam', hands: 'webcam', pose: 'webcam', face: 'webcam', mediapipe: 'webcam', mocap: 'webcam', capturecard: 'webcam', silhouette: 'webcam', segmentation: 'webcam' });
// (round 24) the asset library
TOPICS.assets = `ASSET LIBRARY (round 24): one library of what the owner makes with: textures / pictures, HDRIs, 3D models, fonts, sounds / songs, LUTs (.cube), shaders, presets (and videos), from their watched folders, app downloads (Meshy, Sketchfab…), renders, captures and the free CC0 libraries built in (Poly Haven: HDRIs, textures, models; ambientCG: PBR materials, HDRIs). Files stay where they are (by reference, deduplicated by content hash); tags, favorites and projects on each.
  Tool: three_do { cmd: "asset", op } — find {q (words, kind:hdri, tag:x, fav), kind, limit} → [{id, name, kind, tags}] · use {id, as} (as: picture | channel (the selected ◇ shader's iChannel) | sky | skylayer | model | replace | clips | text {words} | song | footage | sfx | sequence | lut | shader | preset | ref | board; the default by kind: image → a 🖼 picture layer, hdri → the sky (lights the model), model → a 🧊 model layer, font → a 🔤 text layer, sound → the Lab's song, lut → the grade) · free {q, source: polyhaven|ambientcg, type: hdris|textures|models|materials} (search only) · get {source, id, size: 1k|2k|4k, use} (downloads into the library). Prefer the owner's own assets before free ones; a download is CC0 (no credit needed, the page is kept).
  The owner: Ctrl/⌘+Shift+A or /assets (find, use, free, get, watch, tag, fav, import, dupes, preset); drag a card onto the Lab preview, the board, a chat or the sequence.`;
Object.assign(ALIAS, { asset: 'assets', library: 'assets', textures: 'assets', texture: 'assets', hdris: 'assets', fonts: 'assets', font: 'assets', sounds: 'assets', ambientcg: 'assets', pbr: 'assets' });
Object.assign(ALIAS, { shaders: 'shader', glsl: 'shader', shadertoy: 'shader', isf: 'shader', fragment: 'shader', raymarch: 'shader', raymarching: 'shader', feedback: 'shader', multipass: 'shader' }); // (round 23)
// (round 24) the cinematic grade
